h(T, I)` returns, with no template walking (also the residual text and stats). `MA.program(root)` — an algebra operation's CALL CLOSURE (default `M.match`) assembled from the algebra's part files into one mix program, derived from the code (calls and references-as-values followed; `M.x` → `M_x`, file locals → `<file>__x`; a nested function is part of its parent's body). MEASURED 2026-10-02 on the shipped templates — the 33 luajs rewrite rules: every compiled matcher EQUALS A.match on every projected subterm of lua/cartograph its rule is tried on (231,451 subjects, 0 differ), 3.3x faster in all (15.6 s -> 4.7 s; per rule 0.9-15.6x, best of 3 after a full collection); `tests/mixalg_spec.lua` holds 6 rules to A.match on two files' subterms. ⚠ names resolve by name, not scope (a local shadowing a file function) — the equivalence is the guard. CART-1279 |","after":"| COMPILE an algebra template into a plain Lua function — match specialized to the template (the first Futamura projection on the algebra) — and is it the same matcher? | `local MA = require 'cartograph.mixalg'; local m = MA.compile_match(T)` → `m(I)` returns what `A.match(T, I)` returns, with no template walking (also the residual text and stats). `MA.program(root)` — an algebra operation's CALL CLOSURE (default `M.match`) assembled from the algebra's part files into one mix program, derived from the code (calls and references-as-values followed; `M.x` → `M_x`, file locals → `<file>__x`; a nested function is part of its parent's body). MEASURED 2026-10-02 on the shipped templates — the 33 luajs rewrite rules: every compiled matcher EQUALS A.match on every projected subterm of lua/cartograph its rule is tried on (231,451 subjects, 0 differ), 3.3x faster in all (15.6 s -> 4.7 s; per rule 0.9-15.6x, best of 3 after a full collection); `tests/mixalg_spec.lua` holds 6 rules to A.match on two files' subterms. ⚠ names resolve by name, not scope (a local shadowing a file function) — the equivalence is the guard. CART-1279 |\n| May this template FILL that hole — does every instance of it lie in the hole's domain — and what is the composite? (checked composition; STAGING a template = filling a not-yet-known hole with its own UNIT) | `nvim --headless -u NONE -l tools/toolbelt.lua run checked-fill - …` — params are TERMS (tables: `template`, `value`; `hole` a name), so in practice `T.use('checked-fill', { template = T, hole = 'b', value = V })` or `require('cartograph.toolbelt').run(store, 'checked-fill', {…})`: claim holds = ADMITTED and the value carries `composite` (= `A.fill`); refused, `why` names the hole and its domain. The premise is the algebra's own order: `A.admits_template(T, h, V)` = `A.instance_of(V, A.unit(T, h))` — `A.unit(T, h)` is the single-hole template with h's domain (fill(T, h, unit) = T, domains included: the unit law, so staging is composition). ⚠ `A.fill` itself checks NOTHING (measured: a hole pinned to 2 accepts the template 3) — use the tactic where admission matters; `entails` is partial, so a domain it cannot compare is REFUSED, never guessed. Toolbelt params of type `term` (a table with `k` or `body`) and an UNKNOWN declared type now refuse by name (it used to pass the value through unchecked). 7 examples; 3/3 mutations caught. CART-1342 |","file":".claude/skills/cartograph/references/questions.md"},"verb":"edit"},"bytes":1283,"decided_by":"tactic","id":"1790987070.159909-edit"}],"hand":0,"open_intents":[]}
